---
import { mastheadStamp } from "../../dossiers/headline.js";
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import EveryCut from '../../components/EveryCut.astro';
import GuideData from '../../components/GuideData.astro';
import {bySlug,kicker} from '../../dossiers/registry.js';
import {dateStamp} from '../../dossiers/display.js';
import {schoolModel} from '../../dossiers/schools.model.js';
const D=bySlug.schools;
const model=schoolModel();
const groups=[...new Set(model.plates.map(p=>p.group))];
const period=model.plates[0].period;
const cmsePeriod=model.plates.find(p=>p.id==='cmse-23').period;
const payload={facts:model.facts,plates:model.plates.map(({explain,extra,...p})=>p)};
---
<Base title="Schools · India Atlas" description={D.description} nav="guides" dateStamp={mastheadStamp(D)} crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / Education / <b>Schools</b>`} crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`} canonical="https://atlas.thisindianlife.today/guides/schools/" ogImage="/og/schools.png">
<div class="page">
 <div class="titleblock break" data-inputs="schools-national" data-prose="evergreen">
  <div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div>
  <div class="hero-grid"><h1 set:html={D.headline}/><p class="lede">Getting a child into school is the beginning. A teacher, a working toilet, an accessible classroom and a manageable bill help make staying possible. The Ministry of Education’s Unified District Information System for Education Plus (UDISE+) counts schools, pupils and resources. MoSPI’s household education survey asks families what they spend. This guide reads both, with their different populations and definitions kept visible.</p></div>
  <div class="meta"><span>School census <b>{period}</b></span><span>Household survey <b>{cmsePeriod}</b></span><span>School structure <b>Existing class stages</b></span><span>Scope <b>Recognised schools and surveyed households</b></span></div>
  <div class="numbers">
   <div class="num"><div class="l">Enrolled pupils, including school pre-primary</div><div class="v acc" id="n-enrolment">–</div><div class="d">UDISE+, {period}</div></div>
   <div class="num"><div class="l">Schools</div><div class="v" id="n-schools">–</div><div class="d">UDISE+, {period}</div></div>
   <div class="num"><div class="l">Teachers</div><div class="v" id="n-teachers">–</div><div class="d">UDISE+, {period}</div></div>
   <div class="num"><div class="l">School spending per student</div><div class="v" id="n-school_bill">–</div><div class="d">CMSE, current academic year; coaching separate</div></div>
  </div>
 </div>
 <!-- §1 headline above; §2 lived reality -->
 <section data-inputs="schools-national, schools-cmse-33" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>What a school place requires</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>For a family, a school place involves more than a name on a roll. A child needs somebody to teach them, water and usable toilets, and a journey they can make regularly. Course fees are only part of the bill: uniforms, stationery and transport can still cost money when teaching is free.</p><p>The figures here help separate those questions. Counts locate pupils and institutions. Ratios describe access or staffing against a particular population. Facilities describe what schools report having. Household estimates describe expenses borne by enrolled students and their families.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>No single figure is a school-quality score. More teachers do not prove regular attendance or effective instruction. A working computer does not prove that pupils use it. A lower average bill does not prove that a family finds it affordable.</p><p>This guide preserves each measure’s population. Household survey estimates are not used to estimate the national number of pupils. Administrative school counts are not used to infer how many children are outside school.</p></div></div>
 </section>
 <!-- §3 method -->
 <section data-inputs="schools-udise-institution-2, schools-udise-student-2, schools-national, schools-cmse-33" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>How to read these instruments</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="wm-grid"><div><p><b>UDISE+ is an administrative reporting system.</b> Recognised schools supply their enrolment, staffing and facilities. A recorded pupil is enrolled; the count does not establish attendance or learning. The national highlights are taken from the Ministry’s report, checked against the state totals where addition is meaningful.</p><p><b>The collection changed in 2022–23.</b> Earlier rounds collected institution totals; the newer system collected individual student records. The downloadable history is split at that boundary. A change across it combines a change in the measuring instrument with changes in schooling, so it is not presented as one continuous trend.</p><p><b>School stages and ages are different.</b> Gross enrolment includes pupils outside the usual stage age. Net enrolment restricts the age group; adjusted net enrolment also recognises children already at a higher stage. Age-specific enrolment asks whether children of that age are enrolled anywhere.</p><p><b>The household survey is a separate ruler.</b> The Comprehensive Modular Survey: Education (CMSE), collected in April–June 2025, estimates current-academic-year spending and school participation among surveyed households. Coaching is reported separately. Its definitions differ from the earlier NSS education survey, including the treatment of Anganwadis and coaching. These rounds are not joined into a spending trend.</p></div><div class="wm-rail"><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">Read the denominator</span>Per enrolled student, per student reporting an expense, per school and per teacher answer different questions.</aside><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">A median state is not India</span>Where a chart shows a state median, each state/UT receives equal weight. No national rate is computed by averaging state ratios.</aside><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">Counts can overlap</span>Stage totals, school facilities and community classifications can overlap. Add only mutually exclusive components.</aside></div></div>
 </section>
 <!-- §4 current reading: data-bound figures from the deterministic facts sheet -->
 <section id="current-reading" data-inputs="schools-national, schools-cmse-33" data-prose="data-bound facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>The current reading, with the limits attached</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">{period} and {cmsePeriod}</span></div>
  <div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>The later stages reach a smaller share of their age groups. The national gross enrolment ratio is <b id="n-secondary_ger">–</b> at secondary level and <b id="n-higher_ger">–</b> at higher secondary. These are separate stages, not a cohort followed through time.</p><p>The national secondary dropout rate is <b id="n-dropout">–</b>. It is a calculated flow measure; it does not identify why a particular child stopped attending.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>The household-spending plates distinguish the bill across all enrolled students from spending conditional on reporting an expense. They also keep the school bill apart from coaching, and separate rural, urban, school-type and stage estimates.</p><p>We do not know from these data whether a child attends regularly, learns well or receives enough support to stay. Those questions require attendance, assessment and household evidence beyond the counts and ratios here.</p></div></div>
 </section>
 <!-- §5 anatomy and §6 granular cuts: each plate declares its bundle and aggregation -->
 {groups.map((group,gi)=><section data-inputs={model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=>p.bundle).join(', ')} data-prose="evergreen explanations and data-bound current values"><div class="sec-head"><h2>{group}</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  {model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=><div class="break"><figure id={'cb-'+p.id} data-inputs={p.bundle}>
   <div class="plate-head"><div class="t"><h3>{p.title}</h3><div class="sub">{p.sub}</div></div><div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id={'pv-'+p.id}>–</div><div class="c">{p.rows[0].name}, {p.period}</div></div></div>
   <div class="frame"><div class="plotzone"><div id={'plot-'+p.id}></div></div><div class="plotfoot"><span class="src">{p.source}, {p.period}. {p.sub}</span><span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href={p.bundle.includes('cmse')?'https://www.mospi.gov.in/':'https://dashboard.udiseplus.gov.in/report2025/'} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span></div></div>
   <div class="explain"><div class="ev"><span class="mk">How to read this</span><p>{p.explain}</p>{p.extra&&<p>{p.extra}</p>}</div><div class="now"><span class="mk">As of {p.period}</span><p>The chart shows {p.period}. The complete source cuts and their own latest observation dates are in the register below. A missing estimate stays missing; historical observations are kept with their original collection instrument.</p></div></div>
  </figure></div>)}
 </section>)}
 <!-- §7 misreadings and provenance -->
 <section data-inputs="schools-national, schools-udise-student-31, schools-cmse-33, schools-cmse-40" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Common misreadings</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p><b>“Enrolment is attendance.”</b> An entry on a roll cannot establish whether the child attends regularly. Nor does it measure learning.</p><p><b>“The pupil-teacher ratio is class size.”</b> It averages pupils and teachers across the reported system. Schools can still have uneven staffing and assignments.</p><p><b>“A facility count proves access.”</b> Presence, functionality, adequacy and actual use are separate questions. Accessibility can differ across pupils in the same school.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p><b>“The average school bill is what a typical family pays.”</b> The survey reports means per student, with specific expense and reporting bases. Means do not describe the median family or the distribution of affordability.</p><p><b>“The survey totals can be multiplied into student counts.”</b> MoSPI explicitly limits those totals to producing rates and ratios. They are not definitive population counts.</p><p><b>“A national change across collection methods is a dropout count.”</b> The student-record collection boundary changes the instrument. The separate bundles retain it rather than smoothing it away.</p></div></div>
 </section>
 <section data-inputs="schools-national, all schools-udise and schools-cmse bundles" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Sources, method and revisions</h2><span class="line"></span></div><p>UDISE API tables are read at every published row grain, including historical regions, management, school stage, gender, social group, class assignment and facilities. The school collection boundary creates separate bundles. National highlights are extracted from the report’s national table, with its text and document hash archived. Additive counts are checked against state totals; state ratios are never averaged into an India ratio.</p><p>The household survey module is paged in full, with source totals and repeated-row checks. Its school-type shares and spending-component totals are checked independently. State results remain available but are not ranked in the analytical plates because their sample sizes and uncertainty need to accompany interpretation. National school-spending and coaching estimates remain separate.</p><p>The API prints zero gross enrolment ratios for some cells without a usable base. Those ratios are null in the canonical data, with the source zeros retained in the archive. Reported dropout zeros remain source rates and carry the limits explained above. Some social-group GER cells are exceptionally high where population bases are small. One CMSE funding-share cell is negative in the source; it is retained for audit, flagged in the source-quality file and is not used in the analytical charts. The NEP school structure is a separate classification; this guide uses the existing structure consistently.</p><p><a href="https://dashboard.udiseplus.gov.in/report2025/static/media/UDISE+2024_25_Booklet_existing.118ba29d4773e6372f72.pdf">Ministry of Education: UDISE+ existing-structure report</a> · <a href="https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2160863">MoSPI: CMSE release and survey caveats</a></p><p>National report data vintage: <b id="prov-vintage">{model.plates[0].vintage}</b>. All archived and canonical dates are stated separately in the downloads.</p></section>
 <EveryCut slug="schools"/><GuideData slug="schools"/>
</div>
<script is:inline type="application/json" id="schools-data" set:html={JSON.stringify(payload).replace(/</g,'\u003c')}/>
<script>import '../../dossiers/schools.client.js';import '../../dossiers/guide.client.js';</script>
</Base>
